<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="b5bb5719" lines="17-32">
<description>
Fill the background under a top-row red/grey span with a shrinking diagonal pattern that alternates red and grey.

Input structure:
- The grid has an orange background.
- All foreground pixels are red or grey and lie on the top row.
- The first and last top-row foreground pixels define an odd-width span.

Rule:
- Copy the input grid.
- For row r below the top, fill columns span_left + r through span_right - r.
- Each filled cell takes the top-row color r columns up-left, flipped red<->grey once per row step.

Key insight:
- The output is the top-row span propagated down-right along diagonals, while the allowable region shrinks inward from both ends each row.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="833966f4" lines="8-22">
<description>
Swap the upper two-pixel segment and the lower two-pixel segment around the fixed center pixel.

Input structure:
- The grid is a 5x1 vertical column of individual colored pixels.
- The middle pixel is a separator between two same-size two-pixel segments.

Rule:
- Keep the middle pixel in place.
- Reverse the order of the two pixels above the middle.
- Reverse the order of the two pixels below the middle.

Key insight:
- The transformation is local vertical order reversal on each side of the center, not a color-specific rule.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="eval" puzzle_id="f931b4a8" lines="10-27">
<description>
Count the two upper quadrant control regions to size an output made from the lower quadrant pattern.

Input structure:
- The grid is an even square split into four equal quadrants.
- Black is the background.
- The upper-left and upper-right quadrants are control regions.
- The lower-left quadrant is a color source tile; the lower-right quadrant is either a stencil tile or empty.

Rule:
- Output height is the number of non-background pixels in the upper-left quadrant.
- Output width is the number of non-background pixels in the upper-right quadrant.
- If the lower-right stencil has foreground pixels, tile it over the output; each stencil background pixel is filled from the lower-left quadrant cell at the corresponding coarse tile position.
- If the lower-right stencil is empty, tile the lower-left quadrant directly over the output.

Key insight:
- The top quadrants specify only dimensions, while the bottom quadrants specify a two-level repeated pattern: a fine stencil and coarse fill colors.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="7acdf6d3" lines="25-42">
<description>
Move the source-color pixels into the unique outline interior whose size matches the source count.

Input structure
- The grid has a dominant background and exactly two foreground colors.
- One foreground color forms 8-connected outline objects with row-wise interior cells.
- The other foreground color supplies a set of source pixels away from the chosen outline.
Rule
- For each possible outline color, compute each 8-connected outline object's
  row-span interior: background pixels strictly between the leftmost and
  rightmost outline pixels on each occupied row.
- Select the unique outline interior whose cell count equals the total
  number of pixels of the other foreground color.
- Erase the source pixels and paint that matched interior with the source color.
Key insight
- The colors are roles, not fixed IDs: the source color is identified by
  counting, and the destination is the outline with a matching interior area.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="train" puzzle_id="fc4aaf52" lines="17-34">
<description>
Split the two-color object horizontally, swap its colors, and slide the upper half to the right.

Input structure:
- The grid has one background color and exactly two foreground colors.
- All foreground pixels form one 4-connected object with an even-height bbox.
- The object has foreground pixels on both rows adjacent to its horizontal midpoint.

Rule:
- Split the object into upper and lower halves at the midpoint of its bbox.
- Swap the two foreground colors in both halves.
- Keep the lower half in place and shift the upper half right until its lower-left
  boundary pixel is diagonally adjacent to the lower half's upper-right boundary pixel.

Key insight:
- The object is not copied or reflected; it is broken at the horizontal waist,
  recolored by exchanging the two visible object colors, then rejoined diagonally.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="eval" puzzle_id="8f215267" lines="7-21">
<description>
Count same-color patterns per frame and represent the tally as dots inside.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Hollow rectangular frames in various colors.
- Small monochrome pattern sprites scattered outside frames.

Rule:
- Remove all small patterns from the grid.
- For each frame of color C, count how many patterns share color C.
- Draw that many dots along the frame's center row, spaced by 2, starting from the right.

Key insight: Frames act as tally displays — scattered same-color patterns are "absorbed" and their count is shown as interior dots.

